Root cause found and fixed.

**Bug:** In `src/auth.js`, `login(session)` called `getUser(session)` (which just returns `session.user`) and immediately did `user.id` with no null check. Whenever `session.user` is `undefined` — an expired/invalid session, or any session shape without a user attached — that line throws `TypeError: cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'id')`, exactly matching the crash.

**Fix applied** (`src/auth.js`):
```js
function login(session) {
  const user = getUser(session);
  if (!user) {
    throw new Error('No authenticated user for this session');
  }
  return user.id;
}
```
This turns the crash into a clear, catchable error instead of an unhandled `TypeError`, so the login page can show a proper "please sign in again" message rather than crashing.

Note: I couldn't run `git log`/`git blame` in this sandbox (git and shell profile access are blocked here), so I can't confirm exactly what Tuesday's deploy changed — but this is the only `.id` access on the login path in the codebase and matches the error precisely. If you want, I can dig further once shell/git access is available to confirm what changed upstream to start passing sessions without a `user`.
